# Secrets & personal data — never commit credentials or identifying data.
# Auth is delegated to the OS keychain via git/gh; nothing secret should ever
# live in the repo. Everything below is a safety net so a contributor can't
# accidentally commit local tokens, keys, exports, or scratch files. If a
# matched file is genuinely safe to publish, whitelist it explicitly with a
# `!pattern` line or `git add -f` — the friction is the point. Two caveats:
# .gitignore can't protect files that are already tracked (`git rm --cached`
# them first), and it's no substitute for a secret scanner (e.g. gitleaks).

# Environment & local config
.env
.env.*
*.env
.envrc
.direnv/
*.local
secrets.*
*.secret
*.secrets
credentials.*
**/credentials
private/
*.private

